{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794184716,"nonce":"M1susr0U9Ui/Edd/WlZ0y77PYjc=","title":"Modules across files and crates, generics, and the extension trait","message":"use between russl modules, across files and crates. A proc macro cannot read another item, so each module also expands to a hidden macro_rules that hands its token source to a callback; russl! resumes when the tokens arrive. russl_build reads files directly and needs no callback. Only pub items are importable, and only what an entry point reaches is emitted (check, inline, link, split, prune, as HXSL does).\n\nMonomorphised generics over const and scalar type parameters.\n\nAn extension trait with HXSL's hooks: extern functions, a prelude for a family of shaders, a pass over each checked module, builtins, and uniform block and bind group rules. A consumer's framework pieces (for Blinc: the Primitive ABI, the aux data accessors, per-tier variants) live in its extension, not in russl.\n\nDepends on 8fad30d.","files":null}]}